<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex98.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:80%;width:0.0pt;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">// <math id="S3.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathit{in}_{1}}=i..(N-1)\land\iota_{\mathit{in}_{2}}=j..(N-1)\land\iota_{\mathit{out}}=0..k\land i+j=k+1\leq 2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ι</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝑖𝑛</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn></msub></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0.0835em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ι</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝑖𝑛</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0.0835em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ι</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0.0835em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">≤</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathit{in}_{1}}=i..(N-1)\land\iota_{\mathit{in}_{2}}=j..(N-1)\land\iota_{\mathit{out}}=0..k\land i+j=k+1\leq 2N</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="4"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.Ex99"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex99.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">        <math id="S3.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}\land i\leq N\land j\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">≤</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}\land i\leq N\land j\leq N</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="4"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.Ex100"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex100.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.Ex101"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex101.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:80%;width:0.0pt;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">// <math id="S3.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathit{in}_{1}}=\epsilon\land\iota_{\mathit{in}_{2}}=\epsilon\land\iota_{\mathit{out}}=0..(2N)\land k=2N\land i=j=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ι</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝑖𝑛</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">1</mn></msub></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ϵ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ι</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝑖𝑛</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ϵ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">ι</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" lspace="0.0835em" mathcolor="#0000FF" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">k</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∧</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathit{in}_{1}}=\epsilon\land\iota_{\mathit{in}_{2}}=\epsilon\land\iota_{\mathit{out}}=0..(2N)\land k=2N\land i=j=N</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="4"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Translation for a merge program.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Correctness of the Translation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show that the semantics of the program is preserved by the translation,
we define a relation <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle R,H\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}}\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R,H\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}}\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> between
the runtime states of programs before and after the translation as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\begin{array}[]{@{}c@{}}R(x)=R^{\prime}(x)\mbox{ for each $x\mathbin{:}\mathbf{int}\in\Gamma$}\\
\mathit{Idx}(H,a)\supseteq\mathit{setof}(\Theta(a))\mbox{ for each $a$ such that $\Gamma(a)\in\{\mathbf{rarray},\mathbf{warray}\}$}\\
H(a,\Theta(a)[i])=S^{\prime}(a)[i]\mbox{ for each $a$ and $i$ such that}\\
\hskip 113.81102pt\mbox{$\Gamma(a)\in\{\mathbf{rarray},\mathbf{warray}\}$, $0\leq i&lt;|\Theta(a)|$}\\
R^{\prime},H,\Theta\models\mathcal{B}\end{array}}{\begin{array}[]{@{}c@{}}\langle R,H\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}}\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime}\rangle\end{array}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mtext> for each </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">:</mo><mi>𝐢𝐧𝐭</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐼𝑑𝑥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊇</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑠𝑒𝑡𝑜𝑓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for each </mtext><mi>a</mi><mtext> such that </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫𝐚𝐫𝐫𝐚𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰𝐚𝐫𝐫𝐚𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for each </mtext><mi>a</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext> such that</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫𝐚𝐫𝐫𝐚𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐰𝐚𝐫𝐫𝐚𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⊧</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\begin{array}[]{@{}c@{}}R(x)=R^{\prime}(x)\mbox{ for each $x\mathbin{:}\mathbf{int}\in\Gamma$}\\
\mathit{Idx}(H,a)\supseteq\mathit{setof}(\Theta(a))\mbox{ for each $a$ such that $\Gamma(a)\in\{\mathbf{rarray},\mathbf{warray}\}$}\\
H(a,\Theta(a)[i])=S^{\prime}(a)[i]\mbox{ for each $a$ and $i$ such that}\\
\hskip 113.81102pt\mbox{$\Gamma(a)\in\{\mathbf{rarray},\mathbf{warray}\}$, $0\leq i&lt;|\Theta(a)|$}\\
R^{\prime},H,\Theta\models\mathcal{B}\end{array}}{\begin{array}[]{@{}c@{}}\langle R,H\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}}\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime}\rangle\end{array}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(a)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a sequence of indices  of array <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(a)[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(a)[i]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element of the sequence; the sequence describes
how the values of an array <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> of the source program are stored in the stream <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> of
the target program.
For example, if <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}(a)=2\cdot 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}(a)=2\cdot 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(a)=1\cdot 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(a)=1\cdot 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(a,1)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(a,1)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(a,0)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(a,0)=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
To avoid undefined accesses to the heap, we require that the elements of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(a)</annotation></semantics></math> are included in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Idx}(H,a)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{def}}}{{=}}\{m\mid(a,m)\in\mathit{dom}(H)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐼𝑑𝑥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo>=</mo><mtext>def</mtext></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>m</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑑𝑜𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Idx}(H,a)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{def}}}{{=}}\{m\mid(a,m)\in\mathit{dom}(H)\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The last premise <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime},H,\Theta\models\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⊧</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime},H,\Theta\models\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> means that
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> holds under the interpretation which maps (i) each integer and buffer variable <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, (ii) each array <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{m\mapsto H(a,m)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{m\mapsto H(a,m)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(iii) each index sequence variable <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note here that array variables are interpreted by using the state of the program
<em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> the translation, while integer and buffer variables and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> are interpreted by using the state of the
program <em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">after</em> the translation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem on the correctness of the translation states
that the evaluation of source and target programs can be simulated by each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Correctness of translation)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\vdash\{\mathcal{B}\}s\Longrightarrow t\{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\vdash\{\mathcal{B}\}s\Longrightarrow t\{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle R,H\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}}\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R,H\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}}\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R,H,s\rangle\Downarrow\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇓</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R,H,s\rangle\Downarrow\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then
there exist </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime},u\rangle\Downarrow\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇓</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime},u\rangle\Downarrow\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
</span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime},t\rangle\Downarrow\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇓</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R^{\prime},S^{\prime},t\rangle\Downarrow\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then
there exist </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R,H,s\rangle\Downarrow\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇓</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R,H,s\rangle\Downarrow\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given in Appendix <a href="#Pt0.A1" title="Appendix 0.A Correctness of the Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.A</span></a>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R_{1},H_{1}\rangle\sim_{\Gamma\mid\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}\langle R^{\prime}_{1},S^{\prime}_{1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> requires that <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}(x)=R^{\prime}_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}(x)=R^{\prime}_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for each integer variable <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the above theorem says that the results of the programs before and after the translation are the same.
Furthermore, the mutual simulation ensures that the translated program never gets stuck nor diverges if and only if the source program does not.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Automation of Translation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Below we describe how our translation is automated; experimental results are reported
in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiment ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Restriction on Pre-conditions and Post-conditions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the purpose of automating the translation, we have restricted the shape of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
to the form</p>
<table id="S4.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{a_{1}}=[\mathit{low}_{1},\mathit{high}_{1};s_{1}]\land\cdots\land\iota_{a_{k}}=[\mathit{low}_{k},\mathit{high}_{k};s_{k}]\land\varphi," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{a_{1}}=[\mathit{low}_{1},\mathit{high}_{1};s_{1}]\land\cdots\land\iota_{a_{k}}=[\mathit{low}_{k},\mathit{high}_{k};s_{k}]\land\varphi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathit{low}_{i},\mathit{high}_{i};s_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathit{low}_{i},\mathit{high}_{i};s_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sequence of integers from
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{low}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{low}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{high}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{high}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with gap <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
(e.g., <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,6;2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,6;2]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\cdot 2\cdot 4\cdot 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\cdot 2\cdot 4\cdot 6</annotation></semantics></math>),
and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> consists of only (i) buffer information <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=a[e]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=a[e]</annotation></semantics></math> and (ii) conditions on loop variables
(that can automatically be extracted from for-loops; this is the reason why we preferred for-loops over while-loops).
The bounds <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{low}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{low}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{high}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{high}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be linear expressions on free variables
(whose coefficients are automatically inferred as described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Verification Condition Generation ‣ 4 Automation of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described above, our current implementation imposes restrictions on index access patterns, although our formalization does not have such restrictions.
At present, the tool requires that, across loop iterations, the range of accessed indices
shifts by a constant, as in sliding-window or forward/backward scans.
Consequently, it does not support unconventional index orders such as column-major access
(e.g., <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A[j][i]</span> instead of the usual <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A[i][j]</span>) in matrix computations.
In future work, we plan to relax this restriction by fully exploiting the power of the relational Hoare logic,
possibly allowing users to specify loop invariants.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Buffer Insertion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our tool first insert buffering commands as follows.
For each loop and each array accessed in the loop, the tool collect the set of indices (as symbolic expressions)
accessed during each iteration. For example, in the source program of Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Translation Rules ‣ 3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>,
the sets of indices for arrays <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{in}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{out}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,x+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,x+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
The tool order the indices symbolically, and decide that all indices except the largest one should be read from
buffers. On line 13 of Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Translation Rules ‣ 3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the read from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{in}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{in}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> is thus replaced
with a read from buffer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and on line 14, the value read from the corresponding stream is stored in the buffer
for use in the next iteration. In contrast, buffering is unnecessary for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{out}</annotation></semantics></math> since only a single index <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{out}</annotation></semantics></math>
is accessed. (As already explained,
the replacement of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{out}[x]:=z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{out}[x]:=z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}:=z_{1};\mathit{out}.write(b_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}:=z_{1};\mathit{out}.write(b_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is for the sake of simplicity of
translation rules. In the actual implementation, this is replaced directly with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{out}.write(z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{out}.write(z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.)
We also note that, for two-dimensional arrays,
the tool introduce line buffers and apply the same reasoning as above for the first index of each two-dimensional array.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Verification Condition Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the buffers are inserted, to check whether a program can be translated, we can generate verification conditions and check if they are valid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The verification condition generation algorithm typically requires annotations for loop invariants, but in our case, loop invariants are automatically inferred.
This is made possible by the restriction explained in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Restriction on Pre-conditions and Post-conditions ‣ 4 Automation of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.
For each <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{low}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{low}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{high}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{high}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Restriction on Pre-conditions and Post-conditions ‣ 4 Automation of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>,
the tool prepare a linear template
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}+c_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+c_{\ell}x_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}+c_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+c_{\ell}x_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are integer variables occurring in the given
program<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
            In the current implementation, we further restrict <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> to loop variables,
but that restriction is easy to remove.</span></span></span>
and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0},\ldots,c_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0},\ldots,c_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown constants.
For example, for the second loop of Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Translation Rules ‣ 3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the tool prepare the following template
for loop invariant, which we denote <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐼𝑛𝑣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math>:<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
            For simplicity, here
<math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a constant not depending on <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex104"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\iota_{\mathit{in}}=[c_{0,0}+c_{0,1}x,c_{1,0}+c_{1,1}x;c_{2,0}+c_{2,1}x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑖𝑛</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\iota_{\mathit{in}}=[c_{0,0}+c_{0,1}x,c_{1,0}+c_{1,1}x;c_{2,0}+c_{2,1}x]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex105"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\land\iota_{\mathit{out}}=[c_{3,0}+c_{3,1}x,c_{4,0}+c_{4,1}x;c_{5,0}+c_{5,1}x]\land x\neq N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\land\iota_{\mathit{out}}=[c_{3,0}+c_{3,1}x,c_{4,0}+c_{4,1}x;c_{5,0}+c_{5,1}x]\land x\neq N-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly explain how the verification conditions are generated.
As in the standard Hoare Logic, the verification condition is calculated using the approximate weakest precondition <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{awp}(s,\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>awp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{awp}(s,\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined based on the translation rules in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
For example, the approximate weakest precondition for a write to an array is defined by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex106" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex106.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{awp}(a[e]:=x,\mathcal{B})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{def}}}{{=}}e\notin\iota_{a}\land[a\{e\mapsto x\}/a,\iota_{a}\cdot e/\iota_{a}]\mathcal{B}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>awp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>=</mo><mtext>def</mtext></mover><mi>e</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{awp}(a[e]:=x,\mathcal{B})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{def}}}{{=}}e\notin\iota_{a}\land[a\{e\mapsto x\}/a,\iota_{a}\cdot e/\iota_{a}]\mathcal{B}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Note that this is exactly the precondition of <span id="S4.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tr-WriteMem</span>. In the end of the second loop of Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Translation Rules ‣ 3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the (template) invariant <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x+1/x]\mathit{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐼𝑛𝑣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x+1/x]\mathit{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> must hold, and thus, the condition <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{awp}(\mathit{out[x]:=z_{1}},[x+1/x]\mathit{Inv})\equiv x\notin\iota_{\mathit{out}}\land[\iota_{\mathit{out}}\cdot x/\iota_{\mathit{out}},x+1/x]\mathit{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>awp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐼𝑛𝑣</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐼𝑛𝑣</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{awp}(\mathit{out[x]:=z_{1}},[x+1/x]\mathit{Inv})\equiv x\notin\iota_{\mathit{out}}\land[\iota_{\mathit{out}}\cdot x/\iota_{\mathit{out}},x+1/x]\mathit{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> must hold before the statement <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="out[x]:=z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">out[x]:=z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thanks to the shape of the template, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin\iota_{\mathit{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin\iota_{\mathit{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathit{out}}\cdot x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathit{out}}\cdot x</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented as formulas in the theory of (quantified) integer arithmetic.
For instance, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin\iota_{\mathit{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin\iota_{\mathit{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex107" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex107.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lnot\exists d(\mathit{low}_{\mathit{out}}+d\times s_{\mathit{out}}=x\land\mathit{low}_{\mathit{out}}\leq x\leq\mathit{high}_{\mathit{out}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mo>∃</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>𝑙𝑜𝑤</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ℎ𝑖𝑔ℎ</mi><mi>𝑜𝑢𝑡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lnot\exists d(\mathit{low}_{\mathit{out}}+d\times s_{\mathit{out}}=x\land\mathit{low}_{\mathit{out}}\leq x\leq\mathit{high}_{\mathit{out}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">By chaining this backward reasoning, the actual verification condition for the loop is calculated as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Inv\land x&lt;N\implies\mathrm{awp}(s,[x+1/x]\mathit{Inv})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>awp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐼𝑛𝑣</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Inv\land x&lt;N\implies\mathrm{awp}(s,[x+1/x]\mathit{Inv})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the body of the loop.
This condition checks whether <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐼𝑛𝑣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> is a genuine loop invariant.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
            The actual verification condition also contains the entailment among pre-/post-condition and the invariant.</span></span></span>
For the example above,
<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0,0}=c_{0,1}=c_{2,0}=c_{4,1}=c_{5,0}=1,c_{1,0}=N-1,c_{1,1}=c_{2,1}=c_{3,0}=c_{3,1}=c_{5,1}=0,c_{4,0}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0,0}=c_{0,1}=c_{2,0}=c_{4,1}=c_{5,0}=1,c_{1,0}=N-1,c_{1,1}=c_{2,1}=c_{3,0}=c_{3,1}=c_{5,1}=0,c_{4,0}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>
yields the loop invariant as shown on line 12 of Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Translation Rules ‣ 3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and makes the verification condition valid.
Then the tool transform the program according to the translation rules and obtain the program in the right-hand-side of Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.2 Translation Rules ‣ 3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
If the verification condition is unsatisfiable, the tool give up replacing (some of) the arrays with streams,
and keep the arrays as they are.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Implementation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have implemented a prototype tool based on the formalization of our translation presented in
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Formalization of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
As described in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Automation of Translation ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
we have imposed certain restrictions to enable automated translation in the implementation.
To assess the individual contributions of buffer insertion and stream replacement to performance improvement,
our implementation allows the stream replacement phase to be optionally skipped.
In what follows, we refer to the translation that performs only buffer insertion as <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">buffer translation</em>,
and the subsequent stream replacement step as <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stream translation</em>.
We have designed a C-like domain-specific language (DSL) as the source language of our tool,
with C<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;position:relative; bottom:1.7pt;">+</span><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;position:relative; bottom:1.7pt;">+</span> and hardware optimization directives as the target language.
The DSL supports (non-recursive) functions and two-dimensional arrays in addition to the primitives of the source language
presented in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Language ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our proposal, we conduct computing performance measurements on a real FPGA platform using our prototype tool.
We use Z3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> version 4.11.2 as the backend SMT solver to discharge verification conditions.
We use AMD Vitis HLS 2024.1 as the backend compiler of our tool to generate register transfer level (RTL) code from translated C<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;position:relative; bottom:1.7pt;">+</span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;position:relative; bottom:1.7pt;">+</span> source code with directives,
and we use AMD Vivado 2024.1 to generate an FPGA bitstream of the target hardware accelerator.
We use AMD Kria KV260 Vision AI Starter Kit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, an SoC FPGA consisting of a programmable logic (PL) part (i.e., reconfigurable logic) and processing system (PS) part (i.e., CPU cores) as the target FPGA platform.
Without the stream translation, off-chip memory accesses are conducted via the AXI Main (formerly Master) interface of the generated accelerator.
With the stream translation, the AXI Main interfaces are replaced with AXI Stream interfaces, if possible, and the AXI DMA controller transfers the input and output data to the accelerator in a burst manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use PYNQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, a Python-based infrastructure providing software libraries to control hardware accelerators on the programmable logic from the integrated CPU.
The host software code, which consists of a non-kernel part of the translated program, including DMA requests, is executed on the CPU.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following base benchmark programs for the performance measurements:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filter</span> : The motivating example shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filter-Dilated</span> : A variant of <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filter</span> that computes the average of elements at distant indices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Divide</span> : The motivating example shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Relational Hoare Logic for High-Level Synthesis of Hardware Accelerators" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MatVecMul</span> : A program that performs matrix-vector multiplication.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MatAdd</span> : A program that performs addition of two matrices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filter-2D</span> : A version of <span id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filter</span> that processes a two-dimensional array.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stencil-2D</span> : A structured grid computation applying a fixed-size filter over a 2D array.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KMP</span> : A classic string-matching algorithm.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GeMM</span> : Multiplication of two dense matrices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SpMV</span> : Multiplication of a sparse matrix and a dense vector.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the applicability of our framework to realistic workloads,
we include several programs
<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
            We used Stencil-2D, KMP, GeMM, and SpMV from MachSuite.
The remaining MachSuite benchmarks were not included because they fall outside the scope of our transformation.
For example, programs such as AES perform only small, fixed memory lookups without reuse,
so the translated program is identical to the original and provides no additional insight.
</span></span></span>
from MachSuite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, a widely used benchmark suite for accelerator research.
<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
            We also attempted to evaluate existing source-to-source HLS frameworks,
such as Stream-HLS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, as comparison targets.
However, the framework targets different input models (e.g., Torch-MLIR or
restricted affine programs) and could not be applied to our C/C++ benchmarks
in a fully automatic manner.
</span></span></span>
Note that the MachSuite programs are originally written in C<span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;position:relative; bottom:1.7pt;">+</span><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;position:relative; bottom:1.7pt;">+</span>, so we manually simplified them to fit our DSL.
We configure the benchmarks so that their maximum input sizes are at least <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{18}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>18</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{18}</annotation></semantics></math> elements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, to show the capability of the proposal for non-trivial memory access patterns, we consider two variants for each base benchmark program, as described below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rev</span> : A version that reads the input array in reverse order.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Skip</span> : A version in which the loop index is incremented by 2.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">These variants represent non-trivial memory access patterns that can arise in practical applications.
For instance, reverse traversal appears in backward scans (e.g., in dynamic programming or image processing),
and skip-like patterns occur in signal and image processing tasks such as subsampling, decimation, or downsampling.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
